theory.intro:
A box plot is built from **quartiles**, and each quartile marks off $25\%$ of the data. So each whisker and each half of the box covers exactly one quarter of the dataset.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[thick] (0.5,1.6) -- (2.6,1.6);
\draw[thick] (6.2,1.6) -- (8.6,1.6);
\draw[thick] (0.5,1.3) -- (0.5,1.9);
\draw[thick] (8.6,1.3) -- (8.6,1.9);
\draw[thick,fill=orange!20] (2.6,1.0) rectangle (6.2,2.2);
\draw[thick] (4.4,1.0) -- (4.4,2.2);
\node at (3.5,1.6) {$25\%$};
\node at (5.3,1.6) {$25\%$};
\node[above] at (1.55,2.0) {$25\%$};
\node[above] at (7.4,2.0) {$25\%$};
\node[below] at (0.5,1.2) {Min};
\node[below] at (2.6,0.9) {$Q_1$};
\node[below] at (4.4,0.9) {Median};
\node[below] at (6.2,0.9) {$Q_3$};
\node[below] at (8.6,1.2) {Max};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

